\alias{BaseRSpectrumLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Spectral Density Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{spectrum()} as the line it draws: the estimated spectral density
against frequency.

\strong{The values are the raw \code{spec}, not its logarithm.} \code{plot.spec} draws on
a log y axis by default, but it puts the log on the \emph{axis} and hands
\code{plot.xy} the untransformed values -- traced, the first call's \code{y} equals
\code{spectrum(v, plot = FALSE)$spec} exactly. So the numbers a reader hears are
the numbers the chart is scaled from, and taking a logarithm here would
announce a series the caller never computed.

\strong{The caller's arguments are forwarded.} \code{spans}, \code{taper}, \code{detrend} and
the rest change the estimate, so recomputing with defaults would announce a
different curve from the one drawn. The recorded call's arguments are
passed through with \code{plot = FALSE} added.
}
\keyword{internal}
